# CactusFS - what the development cluster of compose.yaml can be told.
#
# Copy this file to .env beside it (git ignores .env; it never enters the
# image) and take the "#" off the lines you set:
#
#   cp deploy/compose/.env.example deploy/compose/.env
#   make up                      # hands the file to compose when it exists
#
# These are exactly the variables compose.yaml reads. The nodes read no file:
# compose puts the values into their environment. A variable exported in the
# shell wins over this file; one that is left out, or set to nothing, leaves
# the node its default. Every other setting of a node (see
# deploy/cactusfs.env.example) is fixed by compose.yaml itself: addresses,
# peers, directories, names and certificates.

# ---- Secrets: NOT in this file ----
#
# Export them in the shell that runs `make up`, from wherever you keep
# secrets. They are listed so that the list is whole, and stay empty here.
#
# The passphrase of the cluster's KMS key file: required, at least 12
# characters, the same at every start.
#   export CACTUSFS_KMS_PASSPHRASE="$(openssl rand -hex 24)"
#CACTUSFS_KMS_PASSPHRASE=
#
# The root credentials of a new deployment: both or neither. Without them one
# node generates a pair and prints it once, into the container log.
#CACTUSFS_ROOT_ACCESS_KEY=
#CACTUSFS_ROOT_SECRET_KEY=

# ---- The nodes ----

# Minimum log level: debug, info, warn or error. Default: info
#CACTUSFS_LOG_LEVEL=info

# Write every audit record to the container log as a JSON line. Default: false
#CACTUSFS_AUDIT_EXPORT=false

# URL (https) of an OTLP/HTTP trace collector the nodes can reach. No default:
# traces are not exported. A credential does not belong in this URL: one with
# userinfo ("user:password@"), a query ("?...") or a fragment ("#...") is
# refused and the nodes do not start. No setting gives the collector a
# credential.
#CACTUSFS_OTLP_ENDPOINT=

# Region name reported to S3 clients. Default: us-east-1
#CACTUSFS_REGION=

# How often certificate and key files are read again; at least 5s, 0 never.
# Default: 1m
#CACTUSFS_TLS_RELOAD_INTERVAL=1m

# How often the downloads a node counted are recorded; at least 5s.
# Default: 30s
#CACTUSFS_ACCESS_FLUSH_INTERVAL=30s

# Pause between two passes of the scrubber; 0 disables it. Default: 24h
#CACTUSFS_SCRUB_INTERVAL=24h

# Limit on how fast the scrubber reads, in MiB per second; 0 is no limit.
# Default: 64
#CACTUSFS_SCRUB_RATE_MIB=64

# Pause between two passes of the lifecycle worker; 0 disables it. Default: 1h
#CACTUSFS_LIFECYCLE_INTERVAL=1h

# Pause between two passes of the garbage collector; 0 disables it.
# Default: 1h
#CACTUSFS_GC_INTERVAL=1h

# Minimum age of an unreferenced shard before it is removed; at least 1h.
# Default: 24h
#CACTUSFS_GC_GRACE=24h

# ---- The engine: not settings of a node ----

# The image the nodes run, and the tag a build here is given.
# Default: cactusfs:dev
#CACTUSFS_IMAGE=cactusfs:dev

# Which build the image says it is. `make up` sets both from the checkout;
# set here they are used by a plain `docker compose up --build`.
#CACTUSFS_BUILD=0
#CACTUSFS_COMMIT=

# The first two digits of the ports of each node on this machine: node 1 is
# reached on <base>00 (S3) and <base>01 (admin, this machine only), node 2 on
# <base>10 and <base>11, node 3 on <base>20 and <base>21, node 4 on <base>30
# and <base>31. Default: 90, which gives 9000, 9010, 9020 and 9030.
#CACTUSFS_PORT_BASE_1=90
#CACTUSFS_PORT_BASE_2=90
#CACTUSFS_PORT_BASE_3=90
#CACTUSFS_PORT_BASE_4=90
